[ShigekiKarita | 2019-01-08T04:31:03Z]
P.S. I think that optimizer's serialization can be similar to module's `register_parameter` way instead of hard coded serialization lines like
```c++
  // these two functions only calls `static void serialize(..)`
  void save(serialize::OutputArchive& archive) const override;
  void load(serialize::InputArchive& archive) override;

  template <typename Self, typename Archive>
  static void serialize(Self& self, Archive& archive) {
    TORCH_OPTIM_SERIALIZE(step_buffers);
    TORCH_OPTIM_SERIALIZE(exp_average_buffers);
    TORCH_OPTIM_SERIALIZE(exp_average_sq_buffers);
    TORCH_OPTIM_SERIALIZE(max_exp_average_sq_buffers);
 }
 // TORCH_OPTIM_SERIALIZE calls torch::optim::detail::serialize and assumes that the variable self is defined
```
This is little bit too nested and complex . I do not want to call `detail` namespace (even wrapped in that macro) when I define my own optimizer because it seems to be private conventionally. https://stackoverflow.com/questions/26546265/what-is-the-detail-namespace-commonly-used-for

---

[ShigekiKarita | 2019-01-09T16:22:42Z]
https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/fe15d6a2c231a7bc1b32781217ed336ccf9adff7/torch/csrc/api/include/torch/optim/serialize.h#L46-L60

I suspect this `emplace_back` just appends tensors to optimizer's buffers without `.clear()` existing tensors. This can result in the constant increasing of memory allocation. However, I do not have time to debug this now. Has anyone reproduced this memory leak yet?

---

[ezyang | 2019-01-10T02:45:40Z]
cc @goldsborough

---

[goldsborough | 2019-01-10T16:56:58Z]
https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/pull/15926 fixes the bug. I also moved the serialize functions out of the detail namespace. The macro is really a convenience for the library implementation. I've prefixed it with an underscore to make this clearer. You can just call the function yourself or define your own macro.

The `register_parameter` approach might be viable although optimizers don't just have one type of buffer, but tensor buffers, int buffers, float buffers which would each need to be stored separately. I think a registration-based API would be a little easier on users, but defining your own optimizers is rare enough that I wouldn't rewrite the whole API for this right now.

---

[ShigekiKarita | 2019-01-10T17:05:40Z]
Thanks for the fix. I agree with your thoughts on the API.

body:
I think the [addmm](https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/master/aten/src/TH/generic/THTensorMath.c#L1936) implementation must have a bug.
Actually, I am confused by the the matrix tranpose. If  a matrix with size `m x k`,  why the matrix is transpose if `stride[1] == 1 && LDC_COND(r_->size[1], r_->size[0], r_->stride[0])`? The stride in the second dimension is 1 means that the matrix is no-transpose. do I misunderstand that? Or do I miss some usage rules which are established? Or does the code obey CblasColMajor?

However,  the [comment](https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/master/aten/src/TH/generic/THTensorMath.c#L2007)  conflict with [the other](https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/master/aten/src/TH/generic/THTensorMath.c#L2028). And the corresponding code is also not right.

If I misunderstand the matrix transpose, the code between L2006~L2025 should be below:
```c
  /* m1 */
  /* Need ldm1_ >= max(1, (transpose_m1 == 't' ? k : m)) */
  if(m1->stride[(transpose_r == 'n' ? 0 : 1)] == 1 &&
     m1->stride[(transpose_r == 'n' ? 1 : 0)] >= THMax(1, m))
  {
    transpose_m1 = 'n';
    m1_ = m1;
  }
  else if(m1->stride[(transpose_r == 'n' ? 1 : 0)] == 1 &&
          m1->stride[(transpose_r == 'n' ? 0 : 1)] >= THMax(1, k))
  {
    transpose_m1 = 't';
    m1_ = m1;
  }
  else
  {
    transpose_m1 = (transpose_r == 'n' ? 't' : 'n');
    m1_ = THTensor_(newContiguous)(m1);
    free_m1 = 1;
}
```

